◆58.8% Implementation, 33% Budget Secured&mdash;&ldquo;Huge Gap&rdquo;

Assemblywoman Lee Myung-yeon noted that &ldquo;it&rsquo;s time for pledges made to residents to visibly take shape.&rdquo; Out of 124 pledges from the 8th administration, only 11 have been completed. Sixty-two are ongoing, 46 are in normal progress, and five are partially in progress.

Although the overall implementation rate is 58.8%, only ₩5.3 trillion has been secured out of the ₩16 trillion required, resulting in a budget securing rate of 33%. For the top 10 key pledges, the rate is 32.8%.

Lee pointed out, &ldquo;Among the projects marked as &lsquo;normal&rsquo; or &lsquo;partial,&rsquo; some have no secured budget or implementation plans, showing a large discrepancy with the reported progress.&rdquo;

Governor Kim responded, &ldquo;We are pursuing these projects with annual goals and holding quarterly reviews and implementation reporting sessions to improve execution.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;We will strive to achieve pledges through competitive grant applications and securing national funds.&rdquo;

However, Lee stated, &ldquo;At the current rate of budget securing and project progress, it will be difficult to fulfill promises to residents.&rdquo; She also noted that private sector investment fell significantly short of plans, with the proportion of private funding in the fiscal composition dropping by 13.3 percentage points.

Kim replied, &ldquo;Amid economic uncertainty and rising interest rates, private investment has slowed,&rdquo; and said the province will &ldquo;strengthen infrastructure and institutional cooperation to revitalize private investment.&rdquo;

Lee also raised concerns over the credibility of the pledge modification process. &ldquo;The advisory evaluation panel is reduced to a formality and is used to justify administrative convenience,&rdquo; she said.

As a representative example, she cited the revision of the Jeonbuk Global MICE Promotion Center pledge. Initially, the project included building a MICE industry ecosystem and supporting the construction of a convention center in Jeonju. These were deleted, reducing the project budget from ₩233.6 billion to just ₩3.3 billion. Lee argued, &ldquo;This is not a revision but a de facto withdrawal.&rdquo;

Kim explained, &ldquo;The city of Jeonju is pursuing the project independently. Given the province&rsquo;s fiscal situation, it is more reasonable to operate the initiative through staff reinforcements at the Cultural Tourism Foundation.&rdquo; He added that the advisory evaluation panel &ldquo;includes internal and external experts and conducts formal reviews.&rdquo;

In an interview with this paper, Lee said, &ldquo;The administration announces achievements, but the public does not feel the impact.&rdquo; She warned, &ldquo;Pledges that are only spoken of become false hope.&rdquo; She emphasized, &ldquo;If budget is excluded, the reasons and alternatives should be explained.&rdquo; She concluded, &ldquo;If a pledge is made, seeing it through to the end is the basic responsibility of politics.&rdquo;


