◆No Core Industry or Strategy&mdash;Jeonbuk Lags Behind

Assemblyman Kim Dae-jung criticized the province&rsquo;s continued lag in industrial attraction strategies and execution. He noted that Jeonbuk has never been selected as an exemplary local government under the Ministry of Industry&rsquo;s business relocation support policy.

This year, the Ministry offers up to ₩15 billion per company, with an additional 10% for underdeveloped regions. All 10 Jeonbuk municipalities qualify as such, but none were selected.

Kim stated, &ldquo;Jeonbuk trails behind other regions in all indicators&mdash;attraction performance, job creation, and post-management,&rdquo; calling for a reevaluation of the investment strategy. &ldquo;Even if multiple industries are attracted, there is no representative industry in Jeonbuk,&rdquo; he said, stressing the need to preemptively identify and lead future growth sectors like AI and K-Culture.

Governor Kim responded, &ldquo;Other regions have already secured their leading industries, so Jeonbuk, as a latecomer, is focusing on niche sectors such as defense and biotech.&rdquo;

In a phone interview, Kim Dae-jung said, &ldquo;Borrowing industries already pursued by other provinces and labeling them as niche won&rsquo;t secure Jeonbuk&rsquo;s future.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s time to establish an independent strategy and build a proper industrial foundation.&rdquo;

The numbers&mdash;₩14 trillion in investment agreements, 58.8% implementation rate, and 8.5% investment execution&mdash;may appear promising, but the provincial assembly insists the reality is different. As Jeonbuk remains a latecomer in industrial strategy and continues implementing pledges without adequate budgets or planning, the situation demands concrete strategies and accountable execution to fulfill promises made to residents.
